  "structuring": {
    "prompt_additions": "Trinidad and Tobago uses PART (Roman) > SECTION (continuous Arabic) as the standard structure for Acts. The Constitution uses CHAPTER (Arabic) > PART (Roman) > SECTION — handle with the constitution document class. Some complex acts and SIs add DIVISION (Arabic) between PART and SECTION — flag division headings for post-processing injection of hcontainer wrappers. Basic unit is always SECTION. Subsection (1), Paragraph (a) in italics, Subparagraph (i). Inserted sections carry alphanumeric suffix: 6A, 6B, 6C. Constitutional special-majority acts begin with WHEREAS recitals before the enacting formula — model each as a recital element. Paragraph labels in amendment acts are italicised: (a), (b), (c)."
  },
  "supranational_memberships": [
    {
      "body": "caricom",
      "direct_effect": "none",
      "superseded_subjects": [],
      "notes": "CARICOM law requires domestic transposition (Article 240 Revised Treaty of Chaguaramas 2001). Trinidad and Tobago is a founding CARICOM member (Treaty of Chaguaramas, 1 August 1973). Implements CARICOM obligations via Acts of Parliament (e.g., Caribbean Community Act, Chap. 12:01)."
    },
    {
      "body": "ccj",
      "direct_effect": "none",
      "superseded_subjects": [],
      "notes": "Trinidad and Tobago has NOT acceded to the CCJ's appellate jurisdiction. The Privy Council (London) remains the final appellate court. The CCJ has only original jurisdiction over CARICOM treaty disputes involving TT. This distinguishes TT from Barbados, which transitioned to the CCJ in 2005."
    },
    {
